<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Nicaragua</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Republic of Nicaragua</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Nicaragua, officially the Republic of Nicaragua, is a country in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-america">Central America</a>, bordered by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/honduras">Honduras</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/costa-rica">Costa Rica</a>, with coasts on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caribbean">Caribbean</a> Sea and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pacific-ocean">Pacific Ocean</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spanish-empire">Spanish Empire</a> conquered it in the 16th century, and it gained independence from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> in 1821. Its Caribbean coast, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mosquito-coast">Mosquitia</a>, was a kingdom under British protection until the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-managua">Treaty of Managua</a> of 1860 joined it to Nicaragua. The later history includes the American filibuster <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-walker">William Walker</a>, American occupation, Sandino's <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guerrilla-warfare">guerrilla</a> war, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/somoza-family">Somoza family</a>'s rule from 1936 to 1979, the Nicaraguan Revolution and the Contra War of the 1980s.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/nicaragua.jpg" alt="Nicaragua" width="330" height="198" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: C records · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Nicaragua.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Nicaragua has three geographical regions. The Pacific lowlands are a broad, hot and fertile plain, punctuated by volcanoes such as Mombacho near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/granada">Granada</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/momotombo">Momotombo</a> near León, and they hold over half the population. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-nicaragua">Lake Nicaragua</a>, the largest freshwater lake in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-america">Central America</a>, lies there. The north-central highlands produce <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coffee-economy">coffee</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cattle">cattle</a>, vegetables and gold. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caribbean">Caribbean</a> lowlands, a sparsely populated rainforest, cover 57 per cent of the territory and hold most of the mineral resources, and the Río Coco, the largest river in Central America, forms the border with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/honduras">Honduras</a> there.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paleo-indians">Paleo-Indians</a> lived in the area as early as 12,000 BCE. In later pre-Columbian times the centre and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caribbean">Caribbean</a> coast were home to groups such as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/miskito">Miskito</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rama">Rama</a>, Mayangna and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/matagalpa">Matagalpas</a>, who lived by hunting, gathering, fishing and slash-and-burn agriculture. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christopher-columbus">Christopher Columbus</a> reached the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mosquito-coast">Mosquito Coast</a> in 1502 on his fourth voyage without meeting any Indigenous people. The Spaniards returned twenty years later to the south-west, and the first attempt at conquest was led by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gil-gonzalez-davila">Gil González Dávila</a>, who had arrived in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/panama">Panama</a> in January 1520.</p><p>The Act of Independence of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-america">Central America</a> dissolved the Captaincy General of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guatemala">Guatemala</a> in September 1821, and Nicaragua briefly joined the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-mexican-empire">First Mexican Empire</a>. In July 1823 it entered the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/federal-republic-of-central-america">United Provinces of Central America</a>, and in 1838 it became an independent republic. In 1909 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> supported rebels against President Zelaya, citing differences over the proposed Nicaragua Canal. Warships were sent on 18 November 1909 after 500 revolutionaries, two of them Americans, were executed on Zelaya's order, and he resigned later that year.</p><p>A pact of 1927 engineered by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> created the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/national-guard-panama">Guardia Nacional</a> to replace the marines. Somoza García deposed Sacasa and became president in a rigged election, and his family ruled for 43 non-consecutive years. Carlos Fonseca and two others founded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sandinista-national-liberation-front">Sandinista National Liberation Front</a> in 1961. In 1990 a coalition led by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/violeta-chamorro">Violeta Chamorro</a> defeated the Sandinistas at the polls, which they had not expected.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The capital and largest city is <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/managua">Managua</a>. Spanish is the official language, and groups on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caribbean">Caribbean</a> coast also speak their own languages and English. The population was 7,097,329 in 2026. The country has 15 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/departement">departments</a> and two autonomous regions on the Caribbean coast. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ruben-dario">Rubén Darío</a>, who led the modernismo movement at the end of the 19th century, is regarded as its most important literary figure.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Nicaragua” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>It joined five states, Guatemala, El Salvador, Honduras, <b>Nicaragua</b> and Costa Rica, under a federal government seated in Guatemala City.</blockquote><a href="/empire/federal-republic-of-central-america">Federal Republic of Central America · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The American filibuster William Walker, back in Central America after his expulsion from <b>Nicaragua</b>, was captured by the British and handed to Honduran authorities, who executed him at Trujillo in September 1860.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-honduras">Republic of Honduras · Military</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Nicaragua</b> emerged from the break-up of the Federal Republic of Central America, the union formed by the former provinces of the Captaincy General of Guatemala after independence from Spain in 1821 and from Agustín de Iturbide’s Mexican Empire in 1823.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-nicaragua">Republic of Nicaragua · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Small communities exist in Brazil, Panama and <b>Nicaragua</b>.</blockquote><a href="/religion/rastafari">Rastafari · Spread</a></li><li><blockquote>William Walker in <b>Nicaragua</b> and Narciso López in Cuba were the best known; the Democratic national platform endorsed Walker’s expedition.</blockquote><a href="/ideology/manifest-destiny">Manifest destiny · Spread &amp; Variants</a></li><li><blockquote>In <b>Nicaragua</b> it is a favourite prayer of many peasants and is often carried as a sacramental; under the Somoza government, when campesinos had to carry proof that they had voted for Somoza, they mockingly called that document a &quot;Magnificat&quot;.</blockquote><a href="/songs/magnificat">Magnificat</a></li></ul></section>
